{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "164",
    "case_summary": "A 76-year-old man presented with subacute, progressive, symmetric lower extremity weakness and urinary retention. He had moderate right-sided ptosis that had been present for about 1 month.\n\nExamination demonstrated a severe flaccid quadriparesis and reduced muscle stretch reflexes, which were worse in the lower limbs. Skin examination showed violaceous cutaneous lesions (leukemia cutis). Patient had moderate right-sided ptosis.\n\n- Imaging Studies:    1. MRI brain and orbits: No abnormality in orbits; multiple areas of cutaneous scalp demonstrated restriction of diffusion    2. MRI spine:        - Nodular thickening and enhancement of the lumbosacral nerve roots       - No abnormal signal or enhancement in the spinal cord       - Diffuse heterogeneous T1- and T2-weighted signal abnormalities within the bone marrow       Image Title: MRI Scans of the Lumbosacral Spine       Image Description: A) Axial T2-weighted MRI showing thickened, nodular nerve roots B) Sagittal postcontrast T1-weighted MRI with fat saturation showing contrast enhancement of the nerve roots of the cauda equina    - Laboratory Tests:      Cerebrospinal fluid analysis:      - White blood cell count: 3397/µL      - Red blood cell count: 2000/µL      - Cell differential: 1% lymphocytes, 1% monocytes/macrophages, 98% atypical cells/blasts\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Blastic plasmacytoid dendritic cell neoplasm (BPDCN) with polyradiculopathy\nB. Lymphomatous meningitis\nC. Paraneoplastic polyradiculoneuropathy\nD. Paraneoplastic neurological syndrome\n",
    "root": {
      "label": "Subacute Progressive Symmetric Lower Extremity Weakness with Urinary Retention and Constitutional Alarm Signs",
      "time_course": "subacute",
      "severity": "unspecified",
      "confidence": 0.8,
      "supporting_facts": [
        "progressive symmetric lower extremity weakness",
